# Response headers for the viewer's own host. Cloudflare reads this file from the root of the
# assets directory, the same way it reads lockrot.dev's content/_headers.
#
# There is deliberately no Content-Security-Policy and no X-Frame-Options under /*. Cloudflare
# emits every rule that matches a path rather than letting a narrow one override a broad one:
# measured on a local worker, a request for /frame/ came back carrying both rules' headers, two
# Content-Security-Policy values and two X-Frame-Options. A browser enforces two policies as their
# intersection, so a catch-all here would quietly narrow both policies below into one neither page
# can run under — the frame would be forbidden to its own embedder and forbidden its own scripts.
# The headers under /* are the ones that mean the same thing twice.
#
# Cloudflare Web Analytics is on for the lockrot.dev zone, and its automatic setup injects a beacon
# script (static.cloudflareinsights.com, reporting to cloudflareinsights.com) into every HTML
# response of every host in the zone, this one included. The viewer page and the published reports
# let it in through their policies. The frame's page says no-transform, and the proxy leaves it
# alone (see /frame/ below). no-transform also stops compression, which is why it is on that one
# small page and nowhere else. Cloudflare documents that no-transform disables the injection
# (developers.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/faq/).
# Every rule that matches a path is sent, so a path must get Cache-Control from one rule only:
# scripts/test_viewer_headers.py checks both.
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()

# The viewer page. `connect-src https:` is the one wide directive and it is the feature: a link of
# the form #url=… asks this page to fetch a document from a host the sender named, and the fetch is
# made only after the reader is shown that host and presses the button. It is why the viewer is not
# on lockrot.dev, whose connect-src names three origins and cannot be widened for one path.
# frame-ancestors 'none': this page embeds, it is never embedded.
# The analytics beacon is let in (script-src; connect-src https: already covers where it reports):
# how many people use the viewer is the number worth having. It adds no party to trust — Cloudflare
# serves this page and could change it anyway — and the beacon strips the fragment and the query
# before it reports a URL, so a #data= link does not travel with it (checked in beacon 2026.9.1).
/
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self'; img-src 'none'; connect-src https:; frame-src 'self'; form-action 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# The renderer. This is the prize: a document that arrives from a stranger is rendered by a
# thousand lines of string concatenation, and here that code has no way to reach the network at
# all. connect-src 'none' and img-src 'none' mean a flaw in it cannot carry the reader's
# dependency inventory anywhere; what is left is a page defacing itself, which belongs to whoever
# sent the document already.
#
# 'self' and the origin are both listed, which is not belt and braces for its own sake. The frame
# is sandboxed without allow-same-origin, so its origin is opaque, and whether 'self' still matches
# the document's own URL there is a question the specification leaves thin. Chrome was measured
# doing it — the frame's script ran and greeted the page under `script-src 'self'` — but one
# browser is not the web, and a browser that reads it the other way would leave the renderer
# unable to load its own two files. Naming the origin as well costs nothing and cannot be wrong;
# keeping 'self' is what lets the viewer run under `wrangler dev` on localhost.
#
# style-src needs 'unsafe-inline': the renderer's stylesheet is inlined in the template and the
# markup uses style= attributes, which fall under the same directive. It buys no script execution.
/frame/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' https://viewer.lockrot.dev; style-src 'self' https://viewer.lockrot.dev 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'none'; connect-src 'none'; form-action 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'self' https://viewer.lockrot.dev
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

# The frame's page itself, not its assets: no-transform keeps the analytics beacon out of the one
# place a stranger's document is rendered. Letting it in would take connect-src
# https://cloudflareinsights.com, and that endpoint takes a report under anyone's site token: a
# document that found a flaw in the renderer could post what it renders there under its sender's
# own token and read it in the sender's dashboard. The frame exists to have connect-src 'none'.
# Its page views would only repeat the viewer page's anyway. The renderer files under /frame/ stay
# compressed.
/frame/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate, no-transform

# The site's own published reports, one page per project of a data run. These are `--format=html`
# pages, which carry the renderer and the document inside them, so 'unsafe-inline' is what the
# format is — there is nothing to load from a file and no per-request nonce on a static asset.
#
# What matters is what is not here. `img-src 'none'` and a connect-src that names one origin: the
# documents are built from other people's lock files, and a flaw in the renderer can carry what it
# renders nowhere but to Cloudflare's analytics endpoint. That one origin is there on purpose —
# these are the site's own pages about public projects, and which of them get read is worth
# knowing — so the beacon Cloudflare injects is let in (script-src) and allowed to report
# (connect-src). A released renderer's page carries its own policy as well, and the browser enforces
# both, so scripts/report_page.py adds the same two origins to that one. The page is not sandboxed
# the way the frame is, so a flaw could deface this origin — which holds no cookie, no storage and
# no credential of anyone's, and is the reason these pages are here and not on lockrot.dev.
#
# noindex: these are twenty near-identical shells whose content lives in a JSON script tag and is
# rewritten every week. The page meant to be found is the site's own, which carries the same
# findings as text. scripts/report_page.py writes the same directive into the page itself.
/reports/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'unsafe-inline' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'none'; connect-src https://cloudflareinsights.com; form-action 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
